<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="OpenKey.Gui.Views.Brandmark">

  <!--
    The letter K pulled open: a full-height stem with the arms detached and floating clear of
    it as a chevron, so the letter reads as an opening rather than a closed form.

    Not a key. Every credential manager on Windows — 1Password, Bitwarden, KeePass, Keeper —
    owns a key or keyhole mark in exactly the 16px taskbar slot OpenKey ships into, so a key
    here would not read "AI client", it would read "password manager". It also inverts the
    promise: a key means "I hold something shut", while OpenKey removes an obstacle. The
    negative space between stem and chevron is a keyway profile — the key is present as the
    hole, and is never drawn.

    Geometry on a 24-unit grid: ink box 3–21 on both axes, stroke 3u, arms at 45°, gap 4.44u
    (= 1.5 × stroke, which is what is left over once a full-height 45° chevron and the stem
    are placed against the edges of an 18u box — derived, not chosen). Arm endpoints sit at
    4.06 / 19.94 rather than 4 / 20 because the perpendicular flat cap overshoots vertically
    by 1.5·sin45° = 1.06u; pulling them in lands the tips flush with the stem at y=3 and y=21.
    That costs 0.2° of arm angle, which nobody sees, and buys a flush edge, which everybody
    does. Stroke weight is deliberately identical on both elements, so the mark stays
    redrawable by hand.

    Stroke binds to Brand in all four themes. mono is the proof case and passes trivially:
    Brand goes achromatic, the mark becomes plain foreground, and nothing is lost, because
    the mark never carried meaning in its hue.
  -->
  <Viewbox Stretch="Uniform">
    <Canvas Width="24" Height="24">
      <Path Data="M4.5 3 V21 M11.5 4.06 L19.5 12 L11.5 19.94"
            Stroke="{DynamicResource Brand}"
            StrokeThickness="3"
            StrokeLineCap="Flat"
            StrokeJoin="Round" />
    </Canvas>
  </Viewbox>
</UserControl>
